# Three.js & R3F Guidelines for NullVoid.AI

## 1. GPU Memory Disposal & Traversal
- **Always dispose of geometries, materials, and textures** when components unmount.
- Use recursive traversal helpers (`lib/three-utils.ts`) when removing 3D subtrees to prevent WebGL context leaks.
- Avoid instantiating new `THREE.Vector3`, `THREE.Matrix4`, or `THREE.Color` inside `useFrame` render loops. Reuse static instances outside the component or in `useRef`.

## 2. WebGPU & WebGL Fallback
- Check for WebGPU support using `lib/webgpu-check.ts`.
- Fall back gracefully to WebGL2 renderer if WebGPU is unavailable or unsupported on the client device.

## 3. DOM & Canvas Isolation
- Keep `@react-three/fiber` components inside `components/canvas/`.
- Never mix standard HTML DOM elements inside the R3F `<Canvas>` component directly—use Drei `<Html>` overlay wrapper only when necessary.
- 2D toolbars, sidebars, and overlays must reside in `components/ui/` positioned with CSS `absolute`/`fixed` over the canvas.

## 4. Asset Optimization
- Store `.glb` / `.gltf` 3D models in `public/models/`.
- Ensure GLB models are Draco or Meshopt compressed for web streaming.
- Use `useGLTF.preload` to warm up asset loading before canvas mounting.
